Upstream's version of this lesson wandered off to a throwaway `Tutorial.Accounts.User` with a `full_name` calculation. In this fork the desk you have been building keeps the lesson: you will add derived values to the very `Ticket` and `Representative` resources from lessons 4 to 8, because lesson 10 opens a LiveView on exactly these resources. Nothing new is introduced — only calculated.

## Calculations

[Calculations](https://hexdocs.pm/ash/calculations.html) in Ash allow for displaying complex values as a top level value of a resource.

There are two ways to define one:

* **An `expr/1` calculation** — one expression over attributes, aggregates and other calculations. Ash understands the expression, so it can filter and sort on it, and push it down to the data layer when one is in use.
* **A module calculation** — a module implementing `Ash.Resource.Calculation` (`load/3` to declare what it needs, `calculate/3` to compute in Elixir) for anything an expression cannot say.

Both are declared in a `calculations do ... end` block, loaded with `Ash.load!/2` or the `load:` option, and usable like attributes everywhere else. Exercise 1 below is your first "1" in the 0 / 1 / N sense (lesson 0): date arithmetic beyond the expression catalog, which is exactly what the module form exists for. Exercise 2 stays a single `expr`.

### Exercise 1: how long has that ticket been sitting there?

Every ticket carries a `created_at` timestamp since lesson 4. Derive from it: add a `calculations do ... end` block to `Tutorial.Support.Ticket` with

`calculate :age_in_days, :integer, {Tutorial.Support.Calculations.AgeInDays, []}`

and write the `Tutorial.Support.Calculations.AgeInDays` module next to the resource: `use Ash.Resource.Calculation`, `load/3` declaring that it needs `[:created_at]`, and `calculate/3` returning one age per record — `DateTime.diff(DateTime.utc_now(), t.created_at) |> div(86_400)`. Whole days between then and now is date arithmetic, and the expression catalog has no `date_diff` on current Ash — this is precisely the "when an expression is not enough" door the intro pointed at.

<details class="rounded-lg my-4" style="background-color: #96ef86; color: #040604;">
  <summary class="cursor-pointer font-bold p-4">Show Solution</summary>
  <div class="p-4">

  ```elixir
  defmodule Tutorial.Support.Calculations.AgeInDays do
    use Ash.Resource.Calculation

    @impl true
    def load(_query, _opts, _ctx), do: [:created_at]

    @impl true
    def calculate(records, _opts, _ctx) do
      Enum.map(records, fn t ->
        DateTime.diff(DateTime.utc_now(), t.created_at) |> div(86_400)
      end)
    end
  end
  ```

  and on the Ticket:

  ```elixir
  calculations do
    calculate :age_in_days, :integer, {Tutorial.Support.Calculations.AgeInDays, []}
  end
  ```

  The `{Module, opts}` tuple is the tell: an `expr` calculation declares a formula, a module calculation declares a *program* — with `load/3` saying which attributes it reads.

  </div>
</details>

### Exercise 2: who is overloaded?

Lesson 8 gave every representative a `count_of_open_tickets` aggregate. A number nobody can act on is just trivia; a named judgment is part of the model. Add a calculation to `Tutorial.Support.Representative` that reads like the business:

`calculate :overloaded?, :boolean, expr(count_of_open_tickets > 3)`

Note that a calculation can reference an aggregate — Ash loads what the expression needs.

<details class="rounded-lg my-4" style="background-color: #96ef86; color: #040604;">
  <summary class="cursor-pointer font-bold p-4">Show Solution</summary>
  <div class="p-4">

  ```elixir
  calculations do
    calculate :overloaded?, :boolean, expr(count_of_open_tickets > 3)
  end
  ```

  </div>
</details>

### Enter your solution

```elixir
defmodule Tutorial.Support.Ticket do
  use Ash.Resource,
    domain: Tutorial.Support,
    data_layer: Ash.DataLayer.Ets

  actions do
    defaults [:read]

    # On creation set the representative by providing the id
    create :open do
      accept [:subject, :description, :representative_id]
    end

    update :close do
      accept []
      change set_attribute(:status, :closed)
    end

    update :assign do
      accept [:representative_id]
    end
  end

  attributes do
    uuid_primary_key(:id)
    attribute :subject, :string, allow_nil?: false
    attribute :description, :string, allow_nil?: true

    attribute :status, :atom do
      constraints one_of: [:open, :closed]
      default :open
      allow_nil? false
    end

    create_timestamp :created_at
    update_timestamp :updated_at
  end

  relationships do
    belongs_to :representative, Tutorial.Support.Representative
  end

  code_interface do
    define :assign, args: [:representative_id]
    define :open, args: [:subject, :description, :representative_id]
    define :close, args: []
  end

  # <--- Add the calculations here
end

defmodule Tutorial.Support.Representative do
  use Ash.Resource,
    domain: Tutorial.Support,
    data_layer: Ash.DataLayer.Ets

  actions do
    defaults [:read]

    create :create do
      accept [:name]
    end
  end

  attributes do
    uuid_primary_key :id
    attribute :name, :string
  end

  relationships do
    has_many :tickets, Tutorial.Support.Ticket
  end

  code_interface do
    define :create, args: [:name]
  end

  aggregates do
    count :count_of_open_tickets, :tickets do
      filter expr(status == :open)
    end
  end

  # <--- Add the calculations here
end
```

```elixir
defmodule Tutorial.Support do
  use Ash.Domain

  resources do
    resource Tutorial.Support.Ticket
    resource Tutorial.Support.Representative
  end
end
```

## See the calculations work

Open a ticket and load its age in days. It was created moments ago, so it should be __0__.

```elixir
ticket =
  Tutorial.Support.Ticket
  |> Ash.Changeset.for_create(:open, %{subject: "My spoon is too big!"})
  |> Ash.create!()
  |> Ash.load!([:age_in_days])

ticket.age_in_days
```

Now the representative. Give Joe three open tickets — not enough to be overloaded:

```elixir
joe = Tutorial.Support.Representative.create!("Joe Armstrong")

[
  %{subject: "I can't see my eyes!", representative_id: joe.id},
  %{subject: "I can't find my hand!", representative_id: joe.id},
  %{subject: "My fridge is flying away!", representative_id: joe.id}
]
|> Ash.bulk_create(Tutorial.Support.Ticket, :open, return_records?: true)

joe = Ash.load!(joe, [:count_of_open_tickets, :overloaded?])
joe.count_of_open_tickets
joe.overloaded?
```

The result should be __false__: three open tickets, and the bar sits at *more than* three. Add a fourth and check again:

```elixir
Tutorial.Support.Ticket.open!("My bed is invisible!", nil, joe.id)

joe = Ash.load!(joe, [:count_of_open_tickets, :overloaded?])
joe.count_of_open_tickets
joe.overloaded?
```

The result should be __true__. Nothing recompiled, nothing was stored: the value is derived, every time, from the description.

Note the two forms you just used side by side: `overloaded?` is one expression over an aggregate, so `expr` carries it and Ash can reason about it (filter, sort, push down). `age_in_days` needed plain Elixir, so it is a module — and the price is that Ash treats it as opaque beyond what `load/3` declares. When a calculation starts needing arguments or anything the catalog cannot say, that is the trade you are choosing. More on both inside the [documentation](https://hexdocs.pm/ash/calculations.html).

That completes the desk. You now hold `Tutorial.Support.Ticket` and `Tutorial.Support.Representative` with actions, relationships, aggregates and calculations — the exact domain the next lesson renders as a LiveView.

